      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Given that \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}1,\\, & x\\le 0 \\\\ -2,\\, & x\\text{ }>0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich of the following is the graph of $y=f(x)?$",
      "worked_solution": "We will graph each branch separately.\n\n- On the interval $(-\\infty ,0],$ we have $f(x)=1,$ so the graph is a horizontal line that ends at $(0,1),$ including this point.\n\n- On the interval $(0,\\infty )$, we have $f(x)=-2,$ so the graph is a horizontal line that begins at $(0,-2),$ excluding this point.\n\nTherefore, the graph of the given function is as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14236308/assets/6527b4f7514f14211c796840c704df5544cabfa6099e69a3740e2e1941e126d2.png)",
